WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.270
I'm not quite sure who
they're shooting at,

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.400
'cause you don't use a
main gun to shoot people.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.830
It isn't the most effective
use of that weapon system.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.090
I'm Dr. John Curatola,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.490
a retired Marine Corps
officer of 22 years,

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.750
former history professor,

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.970
and now I'm the resident
military historian

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.430
at The National World War
II Museum in New Orleans.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.040
Today, we're going to look
at World War II battle scenes

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.256
in the movies and determine
how real they actually are.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.610
What we're seeing here
is the amphibious assault

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.370
of the Normandy beaches on June 6, 1944.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.190
Many of those men who came
off those landing craft

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.250
had to traverse up to 100 to 200 yards

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.050
while under enemy fire

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.420
before they would've
even reached the shingle,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.120
which is the main slope
of the beach itself,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.590
and the movie doesn't really depict that.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.056
They basically get off the landing craft,

00:00:54.056 --> 00:00:56.257
and they're already on the
beach or on the shingle.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.800
Some of the obstacles are backwards.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.720
They should be configured 180 degrees

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.460
from the way they are on the beach.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.990
What they're really intended to do

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.880
is to upset a landing craft

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.997
as it comes in under shallow water.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.200
The machine gun, the MG 42,
that the Germans are using,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.152
this is a high-velocity,
high-caliber weapon.

00:01:22.152 --> 00:01:25.200
The problem is they're using
it in very long bursts.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.540
A weapon like that can only
really be effectively used

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.340
in two- to three-second bursts,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.990
otherwise the barrel will burn out,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.850
and then you have a problem
with the weapon itself.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.503
And if you hear the rates
of fire in the movie,

00:01:35.503 --> 00:01:36.493
they're pretty long.

00:01:36.493 --> 00:01:40.200
Shore party. No armor has made it ashore.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.510
We've got no DD tanks on the beach.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.370
Tom Hanks' character is on the radio

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.620
talking to the commander of
the amphibious task force.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.100
A lowly Army captain
talking to an admiral;

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.380
that is way beyond his pay grade.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.990
A company commander is
not calling the commander

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.960
of the amphibious task
force from a field radio

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.400
just to report his position.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.517
But it does show an excellent
portrayal of the confusion.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.655
In the clip, it doesn't
really show how the US forces

00:02:11.655 --> 00:02:13.071
got on top of the high ground,

00:02:13.071 --> 00:02:15.770
off of the shingle and
the beach, and that's OK,

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.426
because depending on
where you landed on D-Day,

00:02:18.426 --> 00:02:20.880
whether you were at Utah or Omaha Beach,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.500
the topography was very different.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.630
Some units had relatively shallow heights

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.890
that they had to scale.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.190
Some had cliff faces, like
the rangers at Pointe du Hoc

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.170
had to literally go up a cliff face

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.580
to get to the German positions.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.400
While it does have a few technical errors,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.700
it's very accurate of the
assault on Normandy Beach,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.334
and I give it an 8 out of 10.

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.033
Oh, s---!

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.020
The Germans are well defended
within the town itself,

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.660
and the Americans have to assault it.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.940
The unit move into the town of Carentan

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.920
into what we call a MOUT environment,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.110
military operations in urban terrain,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.550
which is very difficult.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.800
The danger with an urban environment

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.100
is that the enemy can hide
anywhere and everywhere.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.960
Dog and fox are pulling back.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:07.793
What?

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.580
You have German armor that is

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.580
coming down upon their
defensive positions.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.170
They're showing that the other companies,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.670
D and F, are breaking and running.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.230
Only one company ran.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.270
The other one stayed
there for a long time,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.860
and that company kind of took some umbrage

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.840
with this depiction of them running,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:25.890
and that's not accurate.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.057
Fire, McGrath! Fire!

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:33.990
The bazooka you see used is accurate

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.600
in the fact that they were
down at the tactical level.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.950
Most companies had them,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.860
and so they were distributed
widely while they were engaged.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.890
Armor is soft skinned on its belly.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.730
When its belly is exposed,
you can indeed pierce it.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.980
Well, hello, 2nd Armored.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.190
It isn't the 2nd Armored Division

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.680
that saves the company
in this particular scene.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.180
What happens is Dick Winters,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.740
who serves as the company commander,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.090
calls in mortar support,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.420
and it's the mortars that help
drive off the German armor.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.280
In most tables of organization,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.560
infantry companies and infantry battalions

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.390
have mortars attached to them

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.360
within a weapons company
or some other attachments

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.460
to provide that additional firepower

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.400
for infantry forces that are
relatively lightly armed.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.850
2nd Armored Division
does relieve Easy Company

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.120
later on in the day, but
it isn't like 2nd Armored

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.900
showed up in the nick of time
to save Easy Company's bacon.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.430
This is a very accurate portrayal,

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.030
and I would give this
a 9 out of 10 easily.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.430
In this scene, we're
seeing the British forces

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:44.700
as they evacuate the continent
of Europe at Dunkirk.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.590
You see the men lined up in queues

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.390
in order to get on the
ships, and that's true,

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.970
and many of the pictures of Dunkirk

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.590
see the soldiers indeed doing that.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:53.850
They would get in their lines,

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.130
and they would break apart
if there was an air attack,

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:57.810
and then they would get
back in their queues.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:58.800
One thing I didn't understand

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.870
is why guys would stand
in the water all day

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:01.837
when there's no boat there.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.820
This is a really good depiction

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.640
of what air combat was like
during this part of the war.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.430
You have to lead the enemy target,

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.620
and the German basically
flies into the bullets,

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.540
and that's really how
it's supposed to work.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.860
And one of the things you'll notice,

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.800
he has to do it in short
two-, three-second bursts,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.200
because they only have so much
ammunition in their wings.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.513
They have to be very conservative

00:05:29.513 --> 00:05:31.440
about the rounds that they do fire,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.447
because you only have maybe 10 seconds.

00:05:34.447 --> 00:05:36.230
Yeah, he's down for the count.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.210
The British fighter is jumped

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.280
by the second German fighter.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:42.113
That's accurate.

00:05:42.113 --> 00:05:44.670
This is the days before radar.
There's nothing to hear.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.380
It's too noisy, it's too loud,

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.990
and so as a result, a lot of aerial kills

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.330
in both wars really result from surprise.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.680
What's problematic with that scene

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.200
is Germans don't operate solo.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.550
Usually, they're going to operate

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.550
in two- or four-man
ships working together.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.100
Here you see one enemy 109

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.530
and then followed by another enemy 109,

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.970
and it really don't work that way.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.880
Because this is 80 years after the war,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.410
those really aren't enemy 109s,

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.470
those are actually Hispano 1112 Buchons,

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.290
which are basically
Messerschmitt airframes

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:17.820
with Merlin engines installed in them.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:19.980
But we use them a lot on air shows today

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.425
to represent an enemy 109,

00:06:21.425 --> 00:06:23.050
since there are very few of them

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:24.633
actually in existence today.

00:06:24.633 --> 00:06:26.697
Overall, I'd give this
movie a 7 out of 10.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.050
What we're seeing here in the
movie "Enemy at the Gates"

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.570
is a Russian assault on a German position

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:40.890
in the Battle of Stalingrad.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.740
And so this idea that they
would send troops forward

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.573
without a weapon and with limited rounds

00:06:46.573 --> 00:06:47.823
is a little absurd.

00:06:47.823 --> 00:06:50.490
The Russians had plenty of guns, weapons,

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:51.990
in terms of small arms.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.670
Basically a human-wave tactic.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:57.030
Just throw men into the
mix and see what happens.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.760
And that certainly does
occur in places in history.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.100
However, this idea of just
throwing men nilly-willy

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.340
into a cauldron is certainly
a waste of manpower,

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.530
and it's not something that
they did in large scale.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.120
The way the Russians
actually win this fight

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.530
is they provide a pincher movement

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.450
on the north and the southern edges

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.618
of the city of Stalingrad,

00:07:13.618 --> 00:07:15.510
and they cut off the German 6th Army.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.821
Fall back!

00:07:17.821 --> 00:07:18.712
Fire!

00:07:18.712 --> 00:07:19.545
Fire!

00:07:20.985 --> 00:07:22.680
Another problem with this particular scene

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.680
is once the Soviet assault fails,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:26.850
they're running backwards,

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.620
and then their own forces fire on them

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.230
to prevent them from coming
back into their friendly lines.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.280
They didn't do that either.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.560
Did they certainly shoot individuals

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.300
for desertion and cowardice?

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.670
Sure, absolutely, but not in the manner

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:39.780
that they're depicting here.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.190
They had blocking battalions

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:44.550
that would be used to
keep formations intact

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.770
and to keep them beefed
up on the front lines,

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.170
but not in the manner
that's depicted here.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.020
Certainly you have to pull back,

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:53.111
reconsolidate, reassault.

00:07:53.111 --> 00:07:54.960
That's just a reality of warfare.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.710
So I'd give this a 3 out of 10.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:58.403
Tank!

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:01.279
Get down!

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.110
What we see in this movie, "Defiance,"

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.020
is the Bielski brothers
are Jewish partisans

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.050
who basically defy Nazi occupation

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.360
of the Belarus area where they live.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.850
They use light arms because they can move

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:16.380
in and out relatively quickly.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.000
The Germans will use a lot of vehicles

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.085
that get their troops
from point A to point B,

00:08:20.085 --> 00:08:21.210
and there's many accounts

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.040
of the Jews who are in hiding here

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.765
attacking those kinds of
convoys, but not against armor.

00:08:34.236 --> 00:08:36.090
There's a lot of problems
with this particular scene.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.710
When they're firing the main gun,

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:39.570
I'm not quite sure who
they're shooting at,

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.760
'cause you don't use a
main gun to shoot people.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:44.070
Can you shoot people with it? Absolutely.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.330
But it isn't the kind of thing

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.970
that is the most effective
use of that weapon system.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.010
I think it's supposed
to be a Panzer Mark IV.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.170
Not even close to looking
like a Panzer Mark IV.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.395
The other problem with the
fact that there's a tank there

00:08:54.395 --> 00:08:56.400
is by 1943 and 1944,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.110
the Germans need all
the armor they can get,

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.762
and using it in a rare
action capacity like this,

00:09:00.762 --> 00:09:01.703
it's a problem.

00:09:01.703 --> 00:09:04.080
That armor asset would probably be used

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.880
on the front lines fighting the Russians.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.000
Regarding the gentleman in
the cupola of the turret,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.550
there are two schools of
thought on this, actually.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.680
There are those that
believe the tank commander,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.257
who would sit in the cupola,

00:09:21.257 --> 00:09:24.270
should be up and out so
he can see things better.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.070
However, for the Soviet Union,

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.720
they thought very differently.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:30.750
They wanted to go into battle
buttoned up and protected.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:35.100
So it's a matter on how you
see the tank commander's role.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.460
Is he somebody who should
be just commanding the crew

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.280
and the utilization of that armor piece,

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:45.270
or should he be up and out,
looking around the battlefield,

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.600
and scanning it for potential
targets, for coordination?

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:50.070
Is that an accurate depiction?

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.320
Yeah, probably, depending
on the commander himself

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:53.940
and what risk he can assess.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.280
Given the fact that the
partisans are lightly armed

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.530
with just rifles, a couple of pistols,

00:09:58.530 --> 00:09:59.820
and maybe some hand grenades,

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.680
there's not much they could
really do to this tank.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.290
If they were to button up,
they could easily drive off

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.150
unless there was some kind of obstacle,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:07.650
and that's one thing the brothers did do.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.860
They did fall trees to block roads.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.690
We don't see that in
this particular scene.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.030
I'd probably give this a 4.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:16.275
All right, let's get the boys off.

00:10:16.275 --> 00:10:17.383
Get them off. Get me the last man.

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:19.163
Radio: Watch your f---ing spacing.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.020
There's a couple of problems
here with this scene.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:24.960
First of all, tanks don't
operate independently.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.310
They're not out there on their own.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.770
They're usually part of
a regimental combat team

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.510
or some other larger organization.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:32.970
Again, this idea of combined arms:

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.690
infantry with armor, with artillery,

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:37.980
and maybe with some air power.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.870
And so in this movie you see these tanks

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.180
operating almost as independent elements,

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.380
and armor doesn't work that way.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.340
You see the infantry following
closely behind the armor.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:49.260
That was a tactic,
technique, and procedure

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.180
used early on to follow the armor,

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:54.120
but the problem with that is
you can't talk to each other

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.170
'cause the tanks are loud,

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.282
and you can't talk to the tank commander.

00:10:58.499 --> 00:10:59.605
Get down!

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.160
There's some tactical problems here

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.540
with this particular scene.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.460
The Germans set up a linear ambush,

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.220
but there's no L-shape to this ambush.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.450
An L-shaped ambush is intended
to stop the enemy advancement

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.220
and then attack them from the flanks.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.440
Here, the Germans are just
in a parallel formation

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.050
to the Americans as
they're trolling along.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:23.525
Let's go, let's clean it up.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:29.185
One tank next to the other
tank next to the other tank.

00:11:29.185 --> 00:11:30.410
That's not how they would do it.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:31.710
They would immediately
get into some kind of

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.615
a combat spread, one
providing a base of fire

00:11:34.615 --> 00:11:37.860
with one or two other tanks
moving as a maneuver element

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:42.000
to kind of flank the enemy to
provide additional support.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.560
And if this is a prepared ambush,

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.020
and it looks like it is because
the Germans have dug in,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:47.700
you wouldn't see them
in a linear formation

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:51.240
because, again, it's too
easy to pick off those tanks

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.460
as they're sitting there
in a nice, neat formation.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.930
It looks great for parades.

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:56.297
It doesn't work in combat.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.013
It looks like a laser. It's not.

00:12:03.013 --> 00:12:05.220
The flashes of light you see are tracers.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.500
Usually soldiers will use those

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.720
to help mark where their rounds are going,

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.430
because rounds are basically invisible

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:12.270
while they're in flight.

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:14.910
It's not out of the ordinary to see

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.980
that many tracers going downrange.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:18.540
It does show the grittiness of war.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:20.040
It does show how dirty it was,

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.930
the cramped quarters within a tank itself.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.390
But, again, the tactics
that both the Americans

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:27.180
and the Germans are using
here kind of fall short.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:28.513
I'd give this a 6.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.490
So, when you look at this depiction

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.830
of the Battle of El Guettar,
it really is lacking.

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:42.060
First of all, you got guys

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:43.860
with weapons slung over their shoulders,

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:46.470
they're not even in any kind
of a tactical formation,

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.150
they're just kind of walking around,

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:49.463
and the German army's better than that.

00:12:49.463 --> 00:12:51.382
Even at this stage of the
war, the Germans are still

00:12:51.382 --> 00:12:53.747
a highly disciplined, mobile force.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:58.710
One of the things you see in this movie

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:00.030
is the use of artillery.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:03.030
These puffs of smoke that
you see in the battle itself,

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.550
rounds generally just don't
blow up in the air like that.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.383
Can it happen?

00:13:06.383 --> 00:13:08.670
Yeah, but not to the degree
you see in these movies.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:10.650
There's a lot of air power that occurs

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:11.790
during the Battle at El Guettar

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.950
from both the German and
the American perspective

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.440
that's largely absent in
this particular movie.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.040
I would give this a 3 out of 10.

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:25.770
What we're seeing here is depiction

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:28.860
of the Battle of the Scheldt
in September of 1944,

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:30.930
and it's a fairly accurate engagement,

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:32.820
one, because it's so muddy.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:34.590
Many of these fields are already flooded

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:37.020
by bombing from Allied bombers

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.610
who are destroying some of the dikes,

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.320
and the Germans do some
flooding on their own

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:43.020
to make it an obstacle
for offensive operations,

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:46.560
and so what that leaves is
only a few avenues of approach

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.360
that the Canadian and British forces

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:52.380
can actually cover to
get to these objectives.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.300
And then this is one of the hardest fights

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:56.970
during the Western European campaign.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:59.910
I think they lose up to 13,000 casualties

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:01.510
in the short span of a month.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.660
The Germans have put
their defensive positions

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.730
on these few avenues of approach,

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.760
and so the British army,
the Commonwealth forces

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:17.400
have to go up these
particular avenues of approach

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.770
in order to rid the
Germans of their location.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.920
So it's pretty accurate that
it's a very narrow frontage.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:25.350
You also see the use of British artillery.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:28.710
Keeping in mind too that because
the fields are so flooded,

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:30.360
the use of armor doesn't really

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.550
help the British forces out at all,

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:35.010
and so this really becomes
an infantrymen slog

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:36.510
with some artillery support,

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:38.040
and there's some aviation support.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:40.590
It's not seen in this movie,
but in the actual battle,

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:42.330
the aviation really does help

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:43.963
in this combined-arms integration.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.530
What you see here too is
a German artillery piece.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:51.900
It's an 88-millimeter antitank gun.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:54.090
It is a legendary piece of artillery

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:57.720
that the Germans initially
designed for antiaircraft,

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.210
but then they found that
it's a great antitank weapon,

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:03.450
and if you lower it, and
you see it in that example

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:07.320
as it's traversed down, being
fired parallel to the deck

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:09.870
as opposed to vertically in the sky,

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.330
and the Germans did that to great effect.

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:13.680
I'd give this a 9 out of 10.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:14.998
My favorite World War II video has to be

00:15:14.998 --> 00:15:16.950
"Band of Brothers." The entire series.

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.840
Not only is it technically accurate,

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.150
but it really shows the human cost of war,

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.620
it shows the camaraderie,

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.690
how difficult these conditions were,

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.090
and it does it in a way
that is not only gritty,

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:30.090
but it's also respectful of
these men and their experiences.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:31.980
It probably stands out as my favorite.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.730
If you enjoyed this video,
please click the link above.

